I've been doing it for a very long time  and I enjoy working with people that are  in serious problems and it's a great  thing to be able to give back to society  to go and work I work in some very nice  hospitals in London but to actually go  to a war zone and to go and help people  there is I feel one of the best things I  can possibly do with my life I've done  it since four since 1993 in Bosnia and  I've done it three times a year almost  for the past 23 years but I think that  the most enjoyable things I've been  doing more recently really is to  training and teaching the doctors and  I've used the knowledge that I've gained  over 20 years or so and put it back into  training courses and using all the  videos and pictures of operations that  I've done over those years and actually  now training people how to do it so I  leave a legacy and that's the most  fantastic part of what I do now I was  operating in one of the frontline  hospitals in Aleppo in 2013 and one of  the fighters was in our Hospital who was  shot in the chest and had a chest  trained put in and he went upstairs to  the ward and I went to see him and he  was obviously very unwell and was  becoming very shocked and out of the  chest rain there was about a liter and a  half two liters of blood so he had to go  down to the operating theatre and we had  to do an operation called a thoracotomy  to open his chest and so we did do this  and I was taking through one of the  Syrian the young Syrian surgeons who'd  never done one before and showing him  how to do it and as we were just  starting the operation the doors of the  operating theatre burst open and about  six fighters came in all dressed in  black with them a que forty-sevens and  it was that moment when I suddenly  realized that they were Isis fighters  and we were operating on a an Isis  fighter as well but you have to put all  those things to your back in the back of  your mind and you have to then just  concentrate on the patient themselves  because that patients a human being and  as a humanitarian your job is to help  human beings and that's what we did it's  much worse now when I was in - in 2014  when I was in Aleppo is pretty bad then  there are lots of barrel bombs  being dropped on the eastern part of a  city where I was working  indiscriminately so we were having lots  of civilians children and ladies coming  in very badly injured but the situation  now is absolutely intolerable in the  past month or so or the past six months  when since Russia has been involved  there's basically carpet bombing of  Aleppo City itself the city is such that  there was a bomb being dropped every 17  minutes  I understand last week last weekend was  the worst weekend they'd ever had it had  over 350 people killed a thousand people  injured and of those three and 50 killed  there was 50 children killed as well and  I was sitting at home receiving all  these pictures are from whatsapp of the  injuries that they had they were asking  me things like they'd never seen things  before children with ball bearings in  their various parts of their body and  then they found out that one of these  ball bearings had actually come from a  Russian cluster bomb because it hadn't  exploded and rolled into the hospital  and they opened it up and inside that  was loads of ball bearings so cluster  bombs are being dropped all over a  civilian population and not only that  the hospitals themselves are being  targeted the hospital where we did this  amazing Skype operation three weeks ago  was totally and utterly destroyed last  week with five bombs two rockets and one  of those big bunker bombs which has  totally destroyed it and so there is no  hope then left for the people around  that area and in fact there's only one  or two hospitals left functioning at all  there's not much time left to help these  people we have about six weeks to do  something in because the American  population themselves are now looking  forward to their presidential elections  their diplomacy has broken down  completely between Russia now so un  diplomatically unfortunately have also  broken down so what's left well the only  people that are left are the European  countries and I hope that the United  Kingdom and all the other European  countries together  could put forward a contingent that will  actually go and visit President Putin  many people have told me David it's  pointless doing that but I think we have  to show something we have to show that  we care we have to show that we're not  going to let this happen complete  disregard of international humanitarian  law and Geneva Conventions we cannot  allow it to happen  and we're going to be looked back on in  history I think in the future of being  perhaps complicit with what's going on  because we just didn't do anything and  that's the biggest tragedy of the whole  problem I think they will be held  complicit they knew what was going on  they did absolutely nothing the only  person that's doing something at the  moment is Andrew Mitchell as a member of  the Parliament but everybody else are  just sitting on their hands doing  nothing and unfortunately it would look  like that in a year or so time
 

